1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,990
In February 1916 more than twelve hundred

2
00:00:02,990 --> 00:00:05,469
German guns opened fire on a quiet

3
00:00:05,469 --> 00:00:07,000
French fortress town.

4
00:00:07,141 --> 00:00:08,103
For ten months,

5
00:00:08,103 --> 00:00:10,604
two armies fed nearly three quarters of

6
00:00:10,604 --> 00:00:13,041
a million men into the same few miles.

7
00:00:13,082 --> 00:00:15,366
Germany's top general had a plan that

8
00:00:15,366 --> 00:00:17,835
was not really about capturing ground at

9
00:00:17,835 --> 00:00:18,081
all.

10
00:00:18,123 --> 00:00:19,695
This is the Battle of Verdun,

11
00:00:19,695 --> 00:00:21,972
the battle designed to bleed France white.

12
00:00:22,164 --> 00:00:23,579
And this is how the trap he

13
00:00:23,579 --> 00:00:25,413
set swallowed his own army as well.

14
00:00:26,205 --> 00:00:27,441
By the end of 1915,

15
00:00:27,441 --> 00:00:30,173
the Western Front had frozen into trenches

16
00:00:30,173 --> 00:00:32,254
from the Channel to Switzerland.

17
00:00:33,246 --> 00:00:35,338
Millions of men faced each other across

18
00:00:35,338 --> 00:00:36,410
mud and barbed wire,

19
00:00:36,410 --> 00:00:38,394
and neither side could break through.

20
00:00:39,287 --> 00:00:41,130
In Berlin, the German Chief of the

21
00:00:41,130 --> 00:00:43,081
General Staff, Erich von Falkenhayn,

22
00:00:43,081 --> 00:00:44,436
searched for another way.

23
00:00:45,328 --> 00:00:47,410
He believed the war would be won

24
00:00:47,410 --> 00:00:48,581
or lost in France,

25
00:00:48,581 --> 00:00:50,077
not in Russia or Italy.

26
00:00:50,469 --> 00:00:51,567
Britain, he thought,

27
00:00:51,567 --> 00:00:52,610
was the real enemy,

28
00:00:52,610 --> 00:00:54,642
and France was the sword in Britain's

29
00:00:54,642 --> 00:00:54,916
hand.

30
00:00:55,510 --> 00:00:57,437
Break France, and Britain would have no

31
00:00:57,437 --> 00:00:59,314
ally on the continent strong enough to

32
00:00:59,314 --> 00:00:59,758
fight on.

33
00:01:00,451 --> 00:01:02,109
His answer was not a breakthrough,

34
00:01:02,109 --> 00:01:03,815
but a place the French would defend

35
00:01:03,815 --> 00:01:04,400
at any cost.

36
00:01:04,492 --> 00:01:05,727
That place was Verdun,

37
00:01:05,727 --> 00:01:07,804
an ancient fortress city on the River

38
00:01:07,804 --> 00:01:08,140
Meuse.

39
00:01:08,533 --> 00:01:10,191
Verdun sat in a bulge in the

40
00:01:10,191 --> 00:01:12,560
French line, ringed by forts and pushing

41
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:13,981
into German-held ground.

42
00:01:14,574 --> 00:01:16,844
It had guarded France's eastern frontier

43
00:01:16,844 --> 00:01:19,228
for centuries, and losing it would shatter

44
00:01:19,228 --> 00:01:20,022
French morale.

45
00:01:20,615 --> 00:01:21,330
In his memoirs,

46
00:01:21,330 --> 00:01:23,285
Falkenhayn claimed he had told the Kaiser

47
00:01:23,285 --> 00:01:24,763
he would bleed France to death.

48
00:01:25,656 --> 00:01:27,464
Historians still argue whether that

49
00:01:27,464 --> 00:01:29,014
Christmas memorandum was real,

50
00:01:29,014 --> 00:01:30,305
or written after the war.

51
00:01:30,697 --> 00:01:31,706
Whatever the truth,

52
00:01:31,706 --> 00:01:33,884
his plan relied on German artillery doing

53
00:01:33,884 --> 00:01:34,946
most of the killing.

54
00:01:35,738 --> 00:01:37,836
The Germans called the attack Operation

55
00:01:37,836 --> 00:01:39,288
Gericht, meaning Judgement,

56
00:01:39,288 --> 00:01:40,686
and prepared it in secret.

57
00:01:40,779 --> 00:01:42,281
They massed 1,201 guns,

58
00:01:42,281 --> 00:01:43,914
two thirds of them heavy,

59
00:01:43,914 --> 00:01:46,527
against roughly six hundred French guns.

60
00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:48,603
Trains carried mountains of shells to

61
00:01:48,603 --> 00:01:50,386
depots hidden in the forests north of

62
00:01:50,386 --> 00:01:50,819
the city.

63
00:01:50,861 --> 00:01:53,412
The attack was set for February twelfth,

64
00:01:53,412 --> 00:01:55,517
but fog, rain and snow delayed it

65
00:01:55,517 --> 00:01:56,410
for nine days.

66
00:01:56,902 --> 00:01:58,802
Then, at a quarter past seven on

67
00:01:58,802 --> 00:02:00,405
the morning of February 21,

68
00:02:00,405 --> 00:02:01,651
the guns opened fire.

69
00:02:01,943 --> 00:02:03,113
For about ten hours,

70
00:02:03,113 --> 00:02:05,102
roughly a million shells fell on a

71
00:02:05,102 --> 00:02:07,091
front only thirty kilometres wide.

72
00:02:07,984 --> 00:02:09,798
Woods were shredded into stumps,

73
00:02:09,798 --> 00:02:12,066
and French trenches simply vanished into

74
00:02:12,066 --> 00:02:12,633
the earth.

75
00:02:13,025 --> 00:02:14,223
In the Bois des Caures,

76
00:02:14,223 --> 00:02:16,255
Lieutenant Colonel Émile Driant held on

77
00:02:16,255 --> 00:02:17,974
with two battalions of chasseurs.

78
00:02:18,066 --> 00:02:20,287
Driant was killed on February 22,

79
00:02:20,287 --> 00:02:22,373
and only 118 of his men escaped

80
00:02:22,373 --> 00:02:23,315
from the wood.

81
00:02:24,107 --> 00:02:24,874
In four days,

82
00:02:24,874 --> 00:02:27,058
the Germans pushed forward about four

83
00:02:27,058 --> 00:02:28,356
miles toward the city.

84
00:02:29,148 --> 00:02:31,157
Ahead of them stood Fort Douaumont,

85
00:02:31,157 --> 00:02:33,395
the largest and strongest fort guarding

86
00:02:33,395 --> 00:02:33,796
Verdun.

87
00:02:34,189 --> 00:02:36,129
Its layers of concrete and earth had

88
00:02:36,129 --> 00:02:38,069
been built to withstand the heaviest

89
00:02:38,069 --> 00:02:39,039
shells of the age.

90
00:02:39,230 --> 00:02:40,090
Yet in 1915,

91
00:02:40,090 --> 00:02:42,599
the French had stripped most of its

92
00:02:42,599 --> 00:02:45,179
guns for use elsewhere on the front.

93
00:02:45,271 --> 00:02:46,317
On February 25,

94
00:02:46,317 --> 00:02:49,108
about one hundred German soldiers walked

95
00:02:49,108 --> 00:02:51,271
into the fort almost unopposed.

96
00:02:51,312 --> 00:02:53,248
Inside, they found a warrant officer and

97
00:02:53,248 --> 00:02:54,312
about twenty-five men,

98
00:02:54,312 --> 00:02:55,860
who surrendered without a fight.

99
00:02:56,353 --> 00:02:57,907
In Germany, church bells rang,

100
00:02:57,907 --> 00:02:59,876
and newspapers hailed the capture as a

101
00:02:59,876 --> 00:03:00,601
great victory.

102
00:03:01,394 --> 00:03:03,719
Across France, the loss was a humiliation,

103
00:03:03,719 --> 00:03:05,546
and it would take eight months to

104
00:03:05,546 --> 00:03:06,542
win the fort back.

105
00:03:07,435 --> 00:03:08,297
That same night,

106
00:03:08,297 --> 00:03:10,398
General Philippe Pétain took command of

107
00:03:10,398 --> 00:03:11,583
the defence of Verdun.

108
00:03:12,476 --> 00:03:14,708
He was a careful infantry general who

109
00:03:14,708 --> 00:03:17,001
believed firepower, not courage alone,

110
00:03:17,001 --> 00:03:17,725
won battles.

111
00:03:18,517 --> 00:03:20,121
His first order was simple:

112
00:03:20,121 --> 00:03:21,666
there would be no retreat.

113
00:03:22,558 --> 00:03:24,700
Pétain's real problem was that Verdun had

114
00:03:24,700 --> 00:03:26,580
only one road and one narrow railway

115
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:27,206
to the rear.

116
00:03:27,599 --> 00:03:29,602
That road ran south to the railhead

117
00:03:29,602 --> 00:03:31,833
at Bar-le-Duc, and it became the city's

118
00:03:31,833 --> 00:03:32,348
lifeline.

119
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:35,430
The French gathered about 3,500 trucks and

120
00:03:35,430 --> 00:03:37,489
kept them moving day and night.

121
00:03:37,681 --> 00:03:40,466
By March, one truck passed every fourteen

122
00:03:40,466 --> 00:03:41,960
seconds, carrying men,

123
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:43,930
shells and food to the front.

124
00:03:44,722 --> 00:03:46,262
In its first month,

125
00:03:46,262 --> 00:03:49,424
the road delivered about 263,000 men to

126
00:03:49,424 --> 00:03:50,721
the battlefield.

127
00:03:50,763 --> 00:03:52,865
The soldiers came to call it La

128
00:03:52,865 --> 00:03:54,763
Voie Sacrée, the Sacred Way.

129
00:03:54,804 --> 00:03:57,274
Pétain also rotated divisions in and out

130
00:03:57,274 --> 00:03:59,682
quickly, a system the French called the

131
00:03:59,682 --> 00:04:00,052
noria.

132
00:04:00,845 --> 00:04:02,774
Fresh troops marched up toward the guns

133
00:04:02,774 --> 00:04:04,653
while exhausted survivors trudged back

134
00:04:04,653 --> 00:04:05,493
down beside them.

135
00:04:05,886 --> 00:04:07,716
By May, forty-two French divisions had

136
00:04:07,716 --> 00:04:09,498
already passed through the furnace of

137
00:04:09,498 --> 00:04:09,835
Verdun.

138
00:04:09,927 --> 00:04:10,761
Over the year,

139
00:04:10,761 --> 00:04:13,204
about three quarters of the entire French

140
00:04:13,204 --> 00:04:14,276
Army fought there.

141
00:04:14,968 --> 00:04:16,996
The burden spread across the whole nation,

142
00:04:16,996 --> 00:04:18,879
and almost every French family felt its

143
00:04:18,879 --> 00:04:19,217
weight.

144
00:04:20,009 --> 00:04:20,755
On April 10,

145
00:04:20,755 --> 00:04:22,994
Pétain's order of the day ended with

146
00:04:22,994 --> 00:04:23,864
a famous line:

147
00:04:23,864 --> 00:04:25,357
Courage, we'll get them.

148
00:04:26,050 --> 00:04:27,780
Unable to break through on the east

149
00:04:27,780 --> 00:04:29,559
bank, the Germans widened the attack

150
00:04:29,559 --> 00:04:30,399
across the river.

151
00:04:31,091 --> 00:04:32,935
In March, they struck the west bank,

152
00:04:32,935 --> 00:04:34,933
where French guns had been raking their

153
00:04:34,933 --> 00:04:35,240
flank.

154
00:04:36,132 --> 00:04:38,412
Two hills became killing grounds:

155
00:04:38,412 --> 00:04:40,554
Hill 304, and a ridge called Le

156
00:04:40,554 --> 00:04:42,281
Mort Homme, the Dead Man.

157
00:04:43,173 --> 00:04:45,239
For weeks, the same slopes were taken,

158
00:04:45,239 --> 00:04:46,870
lost and retaken under endless

159
00:04:46,870 --> 00:04:47,522
bombardment.

160
00:04:48,214 --> 00:04:48,805
On May 4,

161
00:04:48,805 --> 00:04:51,434
the Germans seized the northern slope of

162
00:04:51,434 --> 00:04:54,063
Hill 304, but the French held the crest.

163
00:04:54,255 --> 00:04:56,331
Soldiers began to describe the battlefield

164
00:04:56,331 --> 00:04:57,962
as a mill that ground up everyone

165
00:04:57,962 --> 00:04:58,604
sent into it.

166
00:04:59,296 --> 00:05:01,386
Men lived in flooded shell holes,

167
00:05:01,386 --> 00:05:03,476
often cut off from food and water

168
00:05:03,476 --> 00:05:04,046
for days.

169
00:05:04,337 --> 00:05:06,649
Messages often travelled by runner or

170
00:05:06,649 --> 00:05:09,086
carrier pigeon, because telephone wires

171
00:05:09,086 --> 00:05:10,586
were cut almost at once.

172
00:05:11,378 --> 00:05:13,803
In June, the fighting reached Fort Vaux,

173
00:05:13,803 --> 00:05:16,228
the next fortress on the road to Verdun.

174
00:05:16,419 --> 00:05:18,416
Its commander, Major Sylvain-Eugène

175
00:05:18,416 --> 00:05:20,470
Raynal, had taken over the fort only

176
00:05:20,470 --> 00:05:21,268
weeks earlier.

177
00:05:21,460 --> 00:05:22,174
On June 2,

178
00:05:22,174 --> 00:05:25,174
German troops climbed onto the fort's roof

179
00:05:25,174 --> 00:05:27,460
and attacked with flamethrowers.

180
00:05:27,501 --> 00:05:29,053
The battle moved underground,

181
00:05:29,053 --> 00:05:30,926
where men fought from barricades in

182
00:05:30,926 --> 00:05:32,050
pitch-dark corridors.

183
00:05:32,542 --> 00:05:33,738
Soon the water ran out,

184
00:05:33,738 --> 00:05:35,610
and some of what remained turned out

185
00:05:35,610 --> 00:05:36,390
to be poisoned.

186
00:05:36,583 --> 00:05:38,818
Raynal released his last carrier pigeon,

187
00:05:38,818 --> 00:05:40,941
Le Vaillant, with a desperate plea for

188
00:05:40,941 --> 00:05:41,332
relief.

189
00:05:41,624 --> 00:05:43,519
Badly gassed, the pigeon reached the

190
00:05:43,519 --> 00:05:44,414
citadel and died,

191
00:05:44,414 --> 00:05:46,573
later honoured with the Legion of Honour.

192
00:05:46,665 --> 00:05:47,350
On June 7,

193
00:05:47,350 --> 00:05:48,240
out of water,

194
00:05:48,240 --> 00:05:49,610
food and ammunition,

195
00:05:49,610 --> 00:05:51,665
Raynal's garrison surrendered.

196
00:05:51,706 --> 00:05:53,852
Crown Prince Wilhelm was so impressed that

197
00:05:53,852 --> 00:05:55,538
he presented Raynal with a French

198
00:05:55,538 --> 00:05:56,355
officer's sword.

199
00:05:56,847 --> 00:05:59,053
Falkenhayn's plan was failing on its own

200
00:05:59,053 --> 00:06:01,369
terms, because German losses were climbing

201
00:06:01,369 --> 00:06:02,196
almost as fast.

202
00:06:02,788 --> 00:06:04,954
Every French counterattack had to be met

203
00:06:04,954 --> 00:06:05,983
by German infantry,

204
00:06:05,983 --> 00:06:07,337
who died in the same mud.

205
00:06:07,829 --> 00:06:09,652
Still, the German command pressed on

206
00:06:09,652 --> 00:06:11,373
toward the last ridges in front of

207
00:06:11,373 --> 00:06:11,828
the city.

208
00:06:11,870 --> 00:06:12,755
On June 22,

209
00:06:12,755 --> 00:06:16,134
German guns fired more than 116,000 shells

210
00:06:16,134 --> 00:06:18,870
filled with a new gas, diphosgene.

211
00:06:18,911 --> 00:06:20,749
The Germans called it Green Cross,

212
00:06:20,749 --> 00:06:22,911
after the marking painted on each shell.

213
00:06:22,952 --> 00:06:25,289
More than 1,600 French soldiers were

214
00:06:25,289 --> 00:06:27,561
struck down as the gas crept across

215
00:06:27,561 --> 00:06:28,600
their positions.

216
00:06:28,993 --> 00:06:29,751
The next day,

217
00:06:29,751 --> 00:06:31,792
about 80,000 German infantry surged

218
00:06:31,792 --> 00:06:33,833
forward and captured the village of

219
00:06:33,833 --> 00:06:34,241
Fleury.

220
00:06:35,034 --> 00:06:36,784
It was the closest the German army

221
00:06:36,784 --> 00:06:38,483
would ever come to taking Verdun.

222
00:06:39,075 --> 00:06:41,345
That day, General Robert Nivelle issued an

223
00:06:41,345 --> 00:06:42,264
order to his men:

224
00:06:42,264 --> 00:06:43,723
You will not let them pass.

225
00:06:44,116 --> 00:06:46,344
The phrase became They shall not pass,

226
00:06:46,344 --> 00:06:48,455
and it was later wrongly credited to

227
00:06:48,455 --> 00:06:48,865
Pétain.

228
00:06:49,257 --> 00:06:51,335
Fleury changed hands sixteen times that

229
00:06:51,335 --> 00:06:53,200
summer, until almost nothing of the

230
00:06:53,200 --> 00:06:54,106
village remained.

231
00:06:54,198 --> 00:06:54,850
On July 11,

232
00:06:54,850 --> 00:06:57,342
the Germans launched one last major attack

233
00:06:57,342 --> 00:06:58,647
against Fort Souville.

234
00:06:59,239 --> 00:07:01,512
About thirty German soldiers reached the

235
00:07:01,512 --> 00:07:02,421
top of the fort,

236
00:07:02,421 --> 00:07:04,239
less than two miles from Verdun.

237
00:07:04,280 --> 00:07:05,302
They were driven off,

238
00:07:05,302 --> 00:07:07,152
and the German offensive at Verdun had

239
00:07:07,152 --> 00:07:08,028
reached its limit.

240
00:07:08,421 --> 00:07:09,525
Far to the east,

241
00:07:09,525 --> 00:07:11,457
Russia's Brusilov Offensive,

242
00:07:11,457 --> 00:07:12,768
launched on June 4,

243
00:07:12,768 --> 00:07:14,769
smashed into Austria-Hungary.

244
00:07:15,362 --> 00:07:16,350
Then, on July 1,

245
00:07:16,350 --> 00:07:18,822
British and French armies attacked along

246
00:07:18,822 --> 00:07:19,810
the River Somme.

247
00:07:20,403 --> 00:07:22,216
Germany now had to pull guns and

248
00:07:22,216 --> 00:07:24,369
divisions away from Verdun to plug the

249
00:07:24,369 --> 00:07:24,652
gaps.

250
00:07:25,544 --> 00:07:27,670
Falkenhayn ordered his armies at Verdun to

251
00:07:27,670 --> 00:07:29,593
stop attacking and hold what they had.

252
00:07:30,485 --> 00:07:31,285
On August 29,

253
00:07:31,285 --> 00:07:33,563
the Kaiser replaced him with Paul von

254
00:07:33,563 --> 00:07:35,533
Hindenburg and Erich Ludendorff.

255
00:07:36,526 --> 00:07:38,136
Now the French struck back,

256
00:07:38,136 --> 00:07:40,581
led by Nivelle and the aggressive General

257
00:07:40,581 --> 00:07:41,475
Charles Mangin.

258
00:07:41,567 --> 00:07:43,080
They used a creeping barrage,

259
00:07:43,080 --> 00:07:44,854
a wall of shells moving just ahead

260
00:07:44,854 --> 00:07:46,315
of their advancing infantry.

261
00:07:46,608 --> 00:07:47,504
On October 24,

262
00:07:47,504 --> 00:07:49,296
after a six-day bombardment,

263
00:07:49,296 --> 00:07:51,856
French troops recaptured Fort Douaumont.

264
00:07:52,649 --> 00:07:53,460
In two days,

265
00:07:53,460 --> 00:07:55,622
they took more than 6,000 German

266
00:07:55,622 --> 00:07:56,297
prisoners.

267
00:07:56,690 --> 00:07:57,535
On November 2,

268
00:07:57,535 --> 00:07:59,466
the Germans abandoned Fort Vaux,

269
00:07:59,466 --> 00:08:01,639
and it too returned to French hands.

270
00:08:01,731 --> 00:08:04,263
A final French attack began on December

271
00:08:04,263 --> 00:08:06,731
15, behind more than a million shells.

272
00:08:06,772 --> 00:08:10,007
It captured over 11,000 prisoners and 115

273
00:08:10,007 --> 00:08:13,321
guns, and the battle ended on December 18.

274
00:08:13,813 --> 00:08:14,808
After ten months,

275
00:08:14,808 --> 00:08:16,857
the front line stood close to where

276
00:08:16,857 --> 00:08:18,262
it had been in February.

277
00:08:18,854 --> 00:08:21,947
Verdun cost France around 377,000

278
00:08:21,947 --> 00:08:25,603
casualties, and Germany around 337,000.

279
00:08:25,895 --> 00:08:28,670
Together, that is roughly 714,000 men

280
00:08:28,670 --> 00:08:31,445
killed, wounded, missing or captured.

281
00:08:31,936 --> 00:08:33,769
In the first five months alone,

282
00:08:33,769 --> 00:08:35,898
the two armies fired more than forty

283
00:08:35,898 --> 00:08:36,785
million shells.

284
00:08:37,077 --> 00:08:39,054
Falkenhayn had meant to bleed France

285
00:08:39,054 --> 00:08:41,086
white, but Germany had bled almost as

286
00:08:41,086 --> 00:08:41,525
heavily.

287
00:08:42,018 --> 00:08:43,893
Nine villages were wiped off the map,

288
00:08:43,893 --> 00:08:45,667
and six of them were never rebuilt.

289
00:08:46,059 --> 00:08:48,424
Even today, each of those empty villages

290
00:08:48,424 --> 00:08:49,902
keeps an appointed mayor,

291
00:08:49,902 --> 00:08:52,208
caretaker of a place with no residents.

292
00:08:53,100 --> 00:08:56,168
Near the old forts stands the Douaumont

293
00:08:56,168 --> 00:08:58,449
Ossuary, inaugurated in 1932.

294
00:08:59,141 --> 00:09:01,186
It holds the bones of at least

295
00:09:01,186 --> 00:09:03,776
130,000 unidentified French and German

296
00:09:03,776 --> 00:09:04,389
soldiers.

297
00:09:05,182 --> 00:09:07,048
In front of it lie more than

298
00:09:07,048 --> 00:09:09,848
16,000 graves, the largest French military

299
00:09:09,848 --> 00:09:11,181
cemetery of the war.

300
00:09:11,223 --> 00:09:12,742
The battle created heroes,

301
00:09:12,742 --> 00:09:14,787
but some of its lessons would prove

302
00:09:14,787 --> 00:09:15,371
dangerous.

303
00:09:16,264 --> 00:09:19,052
Nivelle's success won him command of the

304
00:09:19,052 --> 00:09:21,910
French Army, but his 1917 offensive ended

305
00:09:21,910 --> 00:09:23,513
in disaster and mutiny.

306
00:09:24,305 --> 00:09:26,399
Pétain was called in to restore order,

307
00:09:26,399 --> 00:09:28,603
and France remembered him as the saviour

308
00:09:28,603 --> 00:09:29,154
of Verdun.

309
00:09:29,346 --> 00:09:31,826
In 1940, that reputation carried him to

310
00:09:31,826 --> 00:09:33,924
power as head of the Vichy regime

311
00:09:33,924 --> 00:09:35,895
that collaborated with Germany.

312
00:09:36,387 --> 00:09:37,967
Verdun was meant to be a machine

313
00:09:37,967 --> 00:09:39,893
for killing Frenchmen while German guns

314
00:09:39,893 --> 00:09:40,535
did the work.

315
00:09:41,428 --> 00:09:43,624
Instead, it became a mill that ground

316
00:09:43,624 --> 00:09:44,514
up both armies,

317
00:09:44,514 --> 00:09:46,176
for almost no ground at all.

318
00:09:46,469 --> 00:09:48,688
For France, holding Verdun became proof

319
00:09:48,688 --> 00:09:50,680
that the nation could suffer almost

320
00:09:50,680 --> 00:09:51,818
anything and endure.

321
00:09:52,610 --> 00:09:55,165
Falkenhayn wanted France to bleed white,

322
00:09:55,165 --> 00:09:56,059
but at Verdun,

323
00:09:56,059 --> 00:09:58,359
both nations bled into the same mud.
